WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:04.471
- On this episode, 
superstar Jewel stops by.

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:07.490
(hip hop music)

00:00:16.366 --> 00:00:18.650
- [Gary] You ask questions,

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.651
and I answer them.

00:00:21.651 --> 00:00:26.606
This is The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:31.948
- Hey everybody, 
this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:33.690
and this is episode 237

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.650
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.139
And I'm excited, Jewel.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.908
I'm super pumped you've 
decided to join the show.

00:00:40.908 --> 00:00:42.342
Thank you so much for coming.

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.777
Jewel and I met the other day.

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:47.750
We had a great kind of 
business chat and hit it off.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.449
She went to the Knicks game.
- Yeah.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:50.817
- And brought 
tremendously bad luck.

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:52.586
That was the worst Knicks game
I've ever been to in my life.

00:00:52.586 --> 00:00:53.420
- That was not me.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.622
It happened to be a star player
that didn't show up and

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:57.524
I didn't make him disappear.
- It's true. It's true.

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.927
But I was really 
excited to do this show.

00:00:59.927 --> 00:01:03.096
Obviously, you guys know I've
gone to the call-in format on

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:04.665
this show but I decided for this

00:01:04.665 --> 00:01:06.400
we'll bring back 
the classic style.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.103
We've got five questions with
Andy over here and before we get

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:11.305
into the questions,--
- Mhmmm.

00:01:11.305 --> 00:01:14.308
- Jewel, for the four people
that may not know who you are,

00:01:14.308 --> 00:01:17.044
why don't you give a little
context of little bit of your

00:01:17.044 --> 00:01:19.046
career and who you are?

00:01:19.046 --> 00:01:20.013
- Sure. My name is Jewel.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.748
It's my real name.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:22.649
These are my real teeth.

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:26.153
Nobody asks me that but I just
thought I'd throw it out there.

00:01:26.153 --> 00:01:28.472
I'm from Alaska.
My family were pioneers.

00:01:28.472 --> 00:01:31.525
I grew up on a homestead which
means I grew up eating only what

00:01:31.525 --> 00:01:34.728
I could kill or can so my 
fang came in really handy.

00:01:34.728 --> 00:01:38.265
It didn't actually. (laughs)

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:39.466
It taught me resilience,

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:42.268
it taught me grit, 
it taught me how to grind.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:45.839
It taught me self-esteem from
the inside out which is being

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:48.008
able to understand that 
you're capable because

00:01:48.008 --> 00:01:49.576
you've been given 
the opportunity to

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.535
struggle which 
is a real blessing.

00:01:51.535 --> 00:01:54.781
And I ended up moving out at 15.

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:57.651
Ended up homeless at 18.
Was shoplifting--

00:01:57.651 --> 00:02:00.020
- Why'd you 
originally move out at 15?

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.321
- My dad was abusive and so

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:04.157
I decided I would just 
live in a cabin by myself.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:07.889
We'd been pretty transient, 
moved around a lot.

00:02:07.889 --> 00:02:10.263
Been taking care of myself quite
a long time and so I wanted to

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:14.634
do it on my own without somebody
in the cabin besides myself.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:15.602
So, too young to drive.

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:18.438
I was hitchhiking in to 
work at a pretty young age.

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:20.220
Working multiple jobs.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.843
- Where?
- In Homer, Alaska.

00:02:22.843 --> 00:02:25.579
And then I got a scholarship
to an amazing art school in a

00:02:25.579 --> 00:02:27.514
roundabout way in Michigan.

00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:28.982
Was able to 
graduate from high school.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:31.270
Yay, me.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.554
Most importantly, if I look back
on my life I knew that at 15

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.323
I should have been a statistic.

00:02:36.323 --> 00:02:39.626
I knew that girls like me, boys
like me even, end up repeating

00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:42.162
the cycle that we're raised
by and I didn't want to be a

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.499
statistic and so my life's
mission became this idea of

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:48.052
nature versus nurture and 
if you received bad nurture

00:02:48.052 --> 00:02:49.970
can get to know 
your real nature?

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.438
And how you re-nurture yourself.

00:02:51.438 --> 00:02:53.240
And so my entire 
life's work has been that.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.043
My songs have been 
a side effect of that.

00:02:56.043 --> 00:02:58.478
And my next chapter of my
life will be more about that.

00:02:58.478 --> 00:02:59.746
- Yeah, and that next chapter

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:02.115
I think is what 
brought us to these two seats.

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:06.019
Obviously, you reached out and
we had a great talk and

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:08.255
I was completely blown away.

00:03:08.255 --> 00:03:10.390
Honestly, after you 
left I said to the guys,

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.325
wow, the 
entrepreneurial understanding,

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:14.194
the emotional intelligence.

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:15.662
So much stuff that, you know,

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:17.798
we come from very 
different backgrounds.

00:03:17.798 --> 00:03:20.367
We have different lives but
there was so much stuff that

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:22.135
you're talking 
about that I was like wow.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:25.505
Like, I get it and I believe
in it and I was really excited

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:26.473
about the meeting.

00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:30.271
I think the self-esteem stuff,
the internal emotional strength.

00:03:30.271 --> 00:03:31.429
- Mhmmm.

00:03:31.429 --> 00:03:34.047
- But what completely blew me
away and why I wanted you on the

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:37.350
show as well is with all the
things like meditation and

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.685
emotional intelligence and a lot

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:39.733
of the things that we
clicked on,--

00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:40.534
- Yeah.

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:42.222
- I was and I just 
want everybody to know,

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:44.191
you know I'm a 
tough ass filter on this,

00:03:44.191 --> 00:03:48.061
I was really taken aback by
your know how of the market and

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:52.332
consumer behavior and business
savviness and it was really

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:53.600
interesting to me.
- Hmmm.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.502
- Do you ever think of yourself,

00:03:55.502 --> 00:03:57.671
do you think of yourself 
as an entrepreneur at all?

00:03:57.671 --> 00:03:59.122
- Yeah, I think 
I've handled my entire

00:03:59.122 --> 00:04:01.124
career very entrepreneurially.

00:04:02.986 --> 00:04:05.712
When I was homeless, I ended up
homeless for about a year when

00:04:05.712 --> 00:04:08.115
I was 18 and I turned 
down the advances of my boss.

00:04:08.115 --> 00:04:09.950
And when I wouldn't 
have sex with him,

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:12.953
he wouldn't give me my paycheck
and so I thought no big deal.

00:04:12.953 --> 00:04:14.688
I'll live in my car 
for a couple months and

00:04:14.688 --> 00:04:16.255
I'll get back on my feet.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.425
But then the car 
I was living in got stolen.

00:04:18.425 --> 00:04:19.926
And so that's how 
I ended up homeless.

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:22.496
And I started 
having panic attacks.

00:04:22.496 --> 00:04:25.638
I began shoplifting a lot and 
I realized I was gonna end up

00:04:25.638 --> 00:04:28.068
in jail or dead in short order.

00:04:28.068 --> 00:04:30.137
And more importantly when 
I was stealing something one day

00:04:30.137 --> 00:04:31.905
I caught an image of 
myself in the mirror and

00:04:31.905 --> 00:04:33.640
I realized I was a statistic.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.641
I didn't beat the odds.

00:04:34.641 --> 00:04:37.277
My goal at 15, three
short years later--

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:37.944
- You were there.

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:40.680
- I was there and so 
I remembered the saying that

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.817
Buddha had said, "Happiness
doesn't depend on who you are or

00:04:43.817 --> 00:04:45.552
"what you have. 
It depends on what you think."

00:04:45.552 --> 00:04:46.787
And so I doubled down on

00:04:46.787 --> 00:04:48.822
figuring out 
what I was thinking.

00:04:48.822 --> 00:04:51.491
And Descartes said, "I think
therefore I am," but if I could

00:04:51.491 --> 00:04:53.226
rephrase that just a 
little bit I would say,

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.462
"I perceive what I 
think therefore I am."

00:04:55.462 --> 00:04:57.898
If you can perceive you're sad,
you're something other than sad.

00:04:57.898 --> 00:04:59.399
If you can 
perceive you have anxiety,

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:00.467
you're something other.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:02.501
We are the observer.

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:03.937
That's an amazing thing.
- Yes.

00:05:03.937 --> 00:05:06.673
- And so I began to 
develop mindfulness exercises to

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:10.043
help me understand and have 
a better relationship with

00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:11.044
showing up now.

00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:13.780
'Cause fear is a thief and it
takes the past and it projects

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.115
it in to the future.
- Sure does.

00:05:15.115 --> 00:05:17.951
- And it robs you of the only
opportunity you have to create

00:05:17.951 --> 00:05:20.053
change and I needed 
to create change or else

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:21.988
I was going to 
go down the toilet.

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.658
So, I began developing 
these mindfulness exercises.

00:05:24.658 --> 00:05:25.592
- What did you call them?

00:05:25.592 --> 00:05:26.560
Because the term mindful

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.161
wasn't really 
around then, right?

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:29.679
What did you them?
Do you remember?

00:05:29.679 --> 00:05:31.598
- I didn't have a word and I 
didn't tell anybody about them.

00:05:31.598 --> 00:05:32.299
- Right.
- I just did them.

00:05:32.299 --> 00:05:35.268
- How did you know about it? 
Had you read something?

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:39.973
What impacted you at that early
of an age to understand to start

00:05:39.973 --> 00:05:41.908
my slang term would 
be hacking the brain?

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:43.243
Putting yourself 
in that mental place,

00:05:43.243 --> 00:05:45.612
I'm so, people always ask me
so many things and I'm always

00:05:45.612 --> 00:05:47.147
stunned on how 
much it's so insular.

00:05:47.147 --> 00:05:47.814
- Mhmmm.

00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:48.915
- I don't consume other stuff.

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:53.053
I don't know but it was so
there but how did you know that?

00:05:54.175 --> 00:05:55.355
- Some of the things 
were just very intuitive.

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:58.758
The exercises I came up were
intuitive but I read a had a

00:05:58.758 --> 00:05:59.926
teacher that started getting

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:02.996
me Greek philosophy 
when I was about 15.

00:06:02.996 --> 00:06:04.231
On the cover of my first album,

00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:06.199
I have a quote on the 
very front of it that says,

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:09.469
"What we call human nature in
actuality is human habit."

00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:12.405
I began to look at the idea
that if my brain is addictive,

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:14.040
can it get 
addicted to healthy things

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.208
instead of negative things?

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:17.911
And studying habit loops and
replacing negative behaviors

00:06:17.911 --> 00:06:19.346
with positive ones.

00:06:19.346 --> 00:06:21.815
So all these exercises I started
developing for myself were

00:06:21.815 --> 00:06:24.584
strictly to get through my day, 
to manage my day anxiety.

00:06:25.845 --> 00:06:26.653
And they worked.

00:06:26.653 --> 00:06:29.889
And at the end of a 
year I felt so much happier.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.124
Even though I was homeless.

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:33.793
And so, when record 
labels started coming to me,

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:35.595
I knew that--
- Why did they come to you?

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:36.863
Did you have something pop?

00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:38.798
Was there anything that sparked

00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:41.001
the first batch of 
people coming to you?

00:06:41.001 --> 00:06:42.335
- I got a gig in a coffee shop.

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:43.970
It was going out of business.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.107
In San Diego at the time, all
of the coffee shops asked you to

00:06:47.107 --> 00:06:49.175
pay them to sing 
there which was shocking.

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:50.510
I grew up bar 
singing with my dad.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:51.845
- I like these 
coffee shop owners.

00:06:51.845 --> 00:06:52.846
(group laughter)

00:06:52.846 --> 00:06:53.546
Go ahead.
- Yeah.

00:06:53.546 --> 00:06:55.215
I grew up singing in 
bars with my dad where

00:06:55.215 --> 00:06:56.950
we got paid something.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.920
And so I was shocked in Southern
California where they wanted you

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:01.187
to pay them for the honor of

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.056
letting people 
talk while you sang.

00:07:03.056 --> 00:07:03.723
- Right.

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:06.526
- So I found, there's actually
a woman who owned a coffee shop

00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:08.461
and I played 
there with my friend.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:11.364
He had a following, I didn't and
at the end I went to settle out

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:12.699
and she goes, "No, 
you keep the tip money."

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:14.267
And I was like, "What 
are you talking about?

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.503
"My friend just 
brought in 200 people?"

00:07:16.503 --> 00:07:18.638
And she goes, 
"You keep the tip money."

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:20.540
I was like, 
"Oh, okay, that's fine.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.709
"Why don't I keep all of 
your food and coffee sales?

00:07:22.709 --> 00:07:24.344
"We'll swap. 
It's all good with me."

00:07:24.344 --> 00:07:27.159
She looked at my 
like I do not like you.

00:07:27.159 --> 00:07:29.482
And she's like, "You can
take the tip money or leave."

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:31.017
I said, "You can keep 
your frickin' tip money."

00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:32.886
And I walked out 
and I cursed her.

00:07:32.886 --> 00:07:35.522
I didn't cuss but I stuck my
finger in her face and I said,

00:07:35.522 --> 00:07:36.623
"You're gonna fail."

00:07:36.623 --> 00:07:38.191
I said, "You're stealing from
the people that are bringing"--

00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:39.959
- You put her out 
of business mentally?

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:41.394
- I put her out 
of business mentally.

00:07:41.394 --> 00:07:42.762
I am a witch. Yes.

00:07:42.762 --> 00:07:45.602
(group laughter) 
You heard it here.

00:07:45.602 --> 00:07:48.568
- Exposed.
- And end.

00:07:48.568 --> 00:07:51.104
Yeah, she was basically stealing

00:07:51.104 --> 00:07:53.707
from people and that 
no artist would go there.

00:07:53.707 --> 00:07:54.374
- Yep.

00:07:54.374 --> 00:07:55.975
- So I found a coffee shop that
was going out of business,--

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:56.643
- Okay.

00:07:56.643 --> 00:07:58.081
- and I said, 
"Before you close your doors,

00:07:58.081 --> 00:08:01.548
"give me two weeks and give me
all the door money and you can

00:08:01.548 --> 00:08:03.983
"have all the coffee 
and sales and food."

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.486
And so we struck a deal and I
suddenly had to go out and get a

00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:09.189
following and I had to write a
bunch of songs 'cause I grew up

00:08:09.189 --> 00:08:11.257
singing cover songs with my dad.

00:08:11.257 --> 00:08:14.160
So I wrote and wrote 
and wrote handed out flyers,

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.130
hustled, I had two people in my
coffee shop that first night.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:17.797
- Of course.

00:08:17.797 --> 00:08:20.066
- And I sang a five hour 
set and I sang my heart out.

00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:23.336
And what I learned is that I was
very lonely and a lot of people

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:26.606
are lonely and I deserved to be
lonely 'cause I only told truth

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:29.255
in one place and it was in a
notebook that nobody read.

00:08:29.255 --> 00:08:33.011
And I decided to take a risk
and be vulnerable and talk about

00:08:33.011 --> 00:08:37.183
what was real and not hide from
the truth and so I poured all my

00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:38.885
feelings out in 
front of these two surfers,

00:08:38.885 --> 00:08:41.020
bless their hearts.
(laughs) And--

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:41.856
- By the way, 
if you're watching,

00:08:41.856 --> 00:08:43.688
either one of these surfers, 
please leave a comment.

00:08:43.688 --> 00:08:45.291
(group laughter)

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:47.254
Holy shit, I was that dude.

00:08:47.254 --> 00:08:49.562
- Yeah. And then the 
next week four people came,

00:08:49.562 --> 00:08:50.530
and the next week seven.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.098
I played there 
every Thursday to try and

00:08:52.098 --> 00:08:54.401
have a very regular time.

00:08:54.401 --> 00:08:56.903
And then after about 
a year, it was sold out.

00:08:56.903 --> 00:08:58.438
People were 
standing outside the window,

00:08:58.438 --> 00:09:01.007
standing in pouring rain for
five hours listening to me

00:09:01.007 --> 00:09:02.409
through a window.
- That's cool.

00:09:02.409 --> 00:09:05.678
- And a radio station put a
bootleg of mine on the air.

00:09:05.678 --> 00:09:10.074
It ended up requested and I got
into the top 10 countdown

00:09:10.074 --> 00:09:11.051
on 91 X in San Diego.

00:09:11.051 --> 00:09:12.819
It's one of the biggest 
stations in the country.

00:09:12.819 --> 00:09:15.155
Labels are like who the heck
is this girl on acoustic guitar

00:09:15.155 --> 00:09:17.657
playing between 
Nirvana and Soundgarden.

00:09:17.657 --> 00:09:18.858
They all started showing up.

00:09:18.858 --> 00:09:20.326
So it was being Cinderella.

00:09:20.326 --> 00:09:22.028
You know, 
limousines are showing up.

00:09:22.028 --> 00:09:23.696
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:09:23.696 --> 00:09:25.999
And so, you'd think I would jump
at the opportunity to have a

00:09:25.999 --> 00:09:29.469
record label or a record
deal except that I just found

00:09:29.469 --> 00:09:31.538
happiness and I was 
not going to give it up.

00:09:31.538 --> 00:09:32.205
- Yeah.

00:09:32.205 --> 00:09:34.674
- And fame is a path so many
people lose their footing on.

00:09:34.674 --> 00:09:35.842
- It's happening to DRock.

00:09:35.842 --> 00:09:37.210
He's changing so much.
- He is.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.145
I just watched him right here.

00:09:39.145 --> 00:09:41.613
Yeah, he's very condescending 
when he stares at me.

00:09:41.613 --> 00:09:44.150
(group laughter)

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.025
- So you were really 
trying to be careful?

00:09:46.025 --> 00:09:47.520
- Yeah, and so I had to

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.189
ask myself some 
very serious questions.

00:09:49.189 --> 00:09:50.256
Why am I doing this?

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:51.291
It's very important to know.

00:09:51.291 --> 00:09:52.495
If you want to end up somewhere,

00:09:52.495 --> 00:09:53.720
you have to know 
where you want to end up.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:56.196
Then you have to have a compass
of knowing how to navigate.

00:09:56.196 --> 00:09:58.565
So for me, was it 
as an artist or famous?

00:09:58.565 --> 00:09:59.966
I knew I wanted to be an artist.

00:09:59.966 --> 00:10:01.401
Nothing against 
fame, by the way.

00:10:01.401 --> 00:10:03.870
It's just which one do you want
as an experience for your life.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.371
I wanted to be an artist.

00:10:05.371 --> 00:10:07.941
So everyday I made decisions
based on being an artist not

00:10:07.941 --> 00:10:09.734
being famous or rich.
- And that's that.

00:10:09.734 --> 00:10:13.113
- And the other kind of 
interesting caveat is I was

00:10:13.113 --> 00:10:16.316
offered a million dollar signing
bonus and I turned it down.

00:10:16.316 --> 00:10:17.283
I had read a book called,

00:10:17.283 --> 00:10:19.152
"Everything You Need To Know
About The Music Business"

00:10:19.152 --> 00:10:20.587
and I learned 
that it was an advance

00:10:20.587 --> 00:10:22.489
and that you 
owed the advance back.

00:10:22.489 --> 00:10:23.823
I knew I was going 
to make a folk record

00:10:23.823 --> 00:10:25.024
at the height of grunge.

00:10:25.024 --> 00:10:28.228
I knew the odds of that working
were incredibly slim and so

00:10:28.228 --> 00:10:31.195
I basically bought my own right
to make my own music and

00:10:31.195 --> 00:10:33.422
I negotiated the biggest 
back-end anybody had

00:10:33.422 --> 00:10:35.568
ever been given in 
royalties and mechanicals.

00:10:35.568 --> 00:10:36.903
- Same thing 
I did with Crush It!

00:10:36.903 --> 00:10:40.206
- Nice. I love it.
- It's so funny.

00:10:40.206 --> 00:10:41.841
- Yeah. Bet on yourself.
- 100%.

00:10:41.841 --> 00:10:42.509
- And I grinded.

00:10:42.509 --> 00:10:43.843
- If you're good 
enough, bet on yourself.

00:10:43.843 --> 00:10:44.544
- Yeah.

00:10:44.544 --> 00:10:46.880
And I turned down reality TV
shows a lot of shortcut things

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.382
but there's no 
shortcut to being great.

00:10:49.382 --> 00:10:52.652
So I did 600, 700 shows a year
and I did it for years and

00:10:52.652 --> 00:10:55.154
I grinded it out and 
ended up breaking through.

00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:57.090
Yes, I've always liked 
myself entrepreneurially.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:58.267
(group laughter)

00:10:58.267 --> 00:10:59.692
To answer your question 
in a really roundabout way.

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:01.461
- It's true, it's great. 
It's really great.

00:11:01.461 --> 00:11:04.211
You know it's funny, I'm sure
these four and a lot of people

00:11:04.211 --> 00:11:07.100
watching are smiling 'cause so
much of what I've been talking

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:08.701
about for the last 
seven, eight years

00:11:08.701 --> 00:11:09.769
has so many of these themes.

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:13.006
- Something I saw on your walls
coming in was about how many

00:11:13.006 --> 00:11:14.574
people come to your funeral.

00:11:14.574 --> 00:11:16.476
I've actually made all 
my decisions in my life,

00:11:16.476 --> 00:11:18.244
what I call my 
deathbed decisions.

00:11:18.244 --> 00:11:20.914
I pretend I'm on my deathbed,
even when I was a young child,

00:11:20.914 --> 00:11:23.650
you know, 17, 18 and said,

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:25.251
"Will this matter 
to me on my deathbed?

00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:26.019
"Is this important?"

00:11:26.019 --> 00:11:29.022
- Is this the best feeling for
you as the person that decided

00:11:29.022 --> 00:11:29.923
hey, these two people,

00:11:29.923 --> 00:11:32.525
do you like are you 
finding a lot of gratitude?

00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:36.062
Are just like so pumped 
in the last 48 hours?

00:11:36.062 --> 00:11:38.665
- [Man] I knew the two of you,
and it's rare to find people

00:11:38.665 --> 00:11:42.769
like this that care about
living life deeply meaningful,

00:11:42.769 --> 00:11:43.469
in a meaningful way.

00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:45.371
And when I hear her 
talk and I hear you talk,

00:11:45.371 --> 00:11:46.139
I love it.

00:11:46.139 --> 00:11:48.207
- Yeah, it's one thing have
some philosophical things.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:51.617
It's another thing to have, 
these guys I know, yeah.

00:11:51.617 --> 00:11:52.412
It's really cool.

00:11:52.412 --> 00:11:54.080
I'm really, really 
glad I got to meet you.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:54.747
- Likewise.

00:11:54.747 --> 00:11:55.702
- Andy, let's do 
the first question.

00:11:55.702 --> 00:11:57.817
- [Andy] Cool.
Carolina asks--

00:11:57.817 --> 00:11:59.285
- [Voiceover] Carolina asks,

00:11:59.285 --> 00:12:03.089
"Jewel, why do you think so many
people struggle to be happy?"

00:12:03.089 --> 00:12:04.857
- Because happiness 
is a byproduct,

00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:06.559
not an actual destination.

00:12:06.559 --> 00:12:09.963
And so people have this
misconception that they're gonna

00:12:09.963 --> 00:12:11.297
find happiness like it's Europe

00:12:11.297 --> 00:12:13.600
and they're never 
gonna move out. (laughs)

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.202
Happiness is a 
byproduct of certain behaviors

00:12:16.202 --> 00:12:17.770
and can set yourself up to win

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:19.739
and you can set 
yourself up to be happy.

00:12:19.739 --> 00:12:22.508
The sad thing is happiness is a
learned skill and a lot of our

00:12:22.508 --> 00:12:24.577
houses don't teach happiness.

00:12:24.577 --> 00:12:26.876
That was the situation 
I was in and so I actually just

00:12:26.876 --> 00:12:28.314
started studying 
people that were happy

00:12:28.314 --> 00:12:30.350
and I saw what 
the algorithm was.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.953
What did they do that's similar
and for me I don't focus on

00:12:33.953 --> 00:12:35.421
happiness as much as harmony.

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:37.924
I don't really believe in the
word balance 'cause balance is a

00:12:37.924 --> 00:12:41.961
binary thing like oof, 
it's a great very tedious.

00:12:43.211 --> 00:12:45.785
Harmony is saying my 
life has many components.

00:12:45.785 --> 00:12:49.165
I'm a woman, 
I'm a mom,

00:12:49.165 --> 00:12:51.604
I am sexual, 
I am spiritual.

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:55.808
I'm a businessperson, all of
those limbs have to have tone

00:12:55.808 --> 00:12:57.076
and that brings about harmony.

00:12:57.076 --> 00:12:58.845
That brings about satisfaction.

00:12:58.845 --> 00:13:00.915
If only have one limb 
that is very buff and

00:13:00.915 --> 00:13:02.388
the rest of us is atrophied,

00:13:02.388 --> 00:13:05.284
we have disharmony,
we have dissatisfaction but

00:13:05.284 --> 00:13:06.386
there's no human school and

00:13:06.386 --> 00:13:07.887
so that's what I'm 
looking at starting.

00:13:07.887 --> 00:13:10.223
I want to be able to teach
people emotional and mindfulness

00:13:10.223 --> 00:13:12.992
skills so that they can 
gain tone in every area.

00:13:12.992 --> 00:13:16.262
- From my standpoint, you know
since Jewel took it up here,

00:13:16.262 --> 00:13:19.298
I will take into one very narrow
place that I've been really try

00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:22.168
to spend a lot of time 
on and that's perspective.

00:13:22.168 --> 00:13:26.205
It's so interesting to me why 
I deem myself happy because

00:13:26.205 --> 00:13:28.341
I just am so grateful.
- Mhmmm.

00:13:28.341 --> 00:13:32.471
- Like the thing we got excited
about of you know the data

00:13:32.471 --> 00:13:35.348
behind being a human 
being is 400 trillion to 1.

00:13:35.348 --> 00:13:38.584
When you just start there and
you realize forget about the

00:13:38.584 --> 00:13:40.953
odds of like beating 
homelessness and like other or

00:13:40.953 --> 00:13:43.723
like being born in a communist
country like just actually

00:13:43.723 --> 00:13:45.795
becoming a human.

00:13:45.795 --> 00:13:46.859
I always make the joke that your

00:13:46.859 --> 00:13:50.338
mom could have had 
another glass of wine.

00:13:50.338 --> 00:13:53.115
Or your dad could have 
been late because of traffic.

00:13:53.115 --> 00:13:55.201
The odds are so insane.

00:13:55.201 --> 00:13:59.010
I'm just so grateful for what 
I have versus what I don't have

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:02.875
and I think people just have all
these admirations and envy and

00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:05.578
all these hard-wiring things.

00:14:05.578 --> 00:14:07.947
To me, it's perspective 
just like there's always,

00:14:07.947 --> 00:14:10.249
I just generally believe there's
always somebody that has it

00:14:10.249 --> 00:14:13.352
worse and the problem is that's
where I default into mentally

00:14:13.352 --> 00:14:15.722
and have practiced 
to put myself there

00:14:15.722 --> 00:14:18.364
versus somebody's got it better.

00:14:18.364 --> 00:14:20.422
- And the importance 
with gratitude is,

00:14:20.422 --> 00:14:22.261
I do a gratitude 
practice every night,

00:14:22.261 --> 00:14:25.098
and when you're grateful, 
you can't be angry,

00:14:25.098 --> 00:14:26.229
you can't be resentful.

00:14:26.229 --> 00:14:28.522
It literally just leaves 
no room for anything else.

00:14:28.522 --> 00:14:29.538
- I'm just grateful.

00:14:29.538 --> 00:14:30.644
Alright, And.
- [Andy] Cool.

00:14:30.644 --> 00:14:31.879
Alan asks,--
- Alan.

00:14:33.213 --> 00:14:34.871
- [Voiceover] Alan asks,
"How do you feel

00:14:34.871 --> 00:14:37.043
"the future of 
music is going to be?

00:14:37.043 --> 00:14:39.145
"How and where do you 
earn most of your money?"

00:14:39.145 --> 00:14:41.614
- I'm actually very excited by
the disrupt in the music space.

00:14:41.614 --> 00:14:45.417
It's deserved to be disrupted
for really quite a long time.

00:14:45.417 --> 00:14:49.051
And this deserved to fail and
I say that with all kindness.

00:14:49.051 --> 00:14:50.707
- You mean the people in the

00:14:50.707 --> 00:14:53.126
middle having 
disproportionate economics?

00:14:53.126 --> 00:14:55.995
- Yeah, if I could do 
a brief history of music.

00:14:55.995 --> 00:14:59.398
Musicians spent a long time
understanding who they were and

00:14:59.398 --> 00:15:00.767
what they offered as musicians.

00:15:00.767 --> 00:15:02.301
People call that a brand now.

00:15:02.301 --> 00:15:03.936
But they were 
natural brand creators.

00:15:03.936 --> 00:15:06.272
And so Led Zeppelin 
stood for something.

00:15:06.272 --> 00:15:08.574
You know, loving a 
musician was like an ethos.

00:15:08.574 --> 00:15:11.511
It was an entire culture and
they were culture builders and

00:15:11.511 --> 00:15:14.514
they spent years cultivating
that culture on the road.

00:15:14.514 --> 00:15:17.016
Radio came along and it 
just super boosted things.

00:15:17.016 --> 00:15:20.119
And there's a golden time there
for when that happened and then

00:15:20.119 --> 00:15:22.255
radio became so 
powerful people realized,

00:15:22.255 --> 00:15:23.956
"Hey, I don't have to 
have a whole great record.

00:15:23.956 --> 00:15:26.793
"I can have one good song," and
then the record labels were like

00:15:26.793 --> 00:15:30.062
and we can charge for an entire
record with only one good song

00:15:30.062 --> 00:15:31.130
and the consumer
started going,

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.299
"Hey, screw you guys. 
I'm getting ripped off.

00:15:33.299 --> 00:15:36.169
"This is a sucky 
record with one good song."

00:15:36.169 --> 00:15:37.970
Enter the digital age 
and people could say,

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.039
"Oh good, I only have 
to buy one good song."

00:15:40.039 --> 00:15:40.873
- At first, they're like,

00:15:40.873 --> 00:15:42.696
"Wait a minute, Napster. 
I'm not buying shit."

00:15:42.696 --> 00:15:44.510
- Yeah.
(group laughter)

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:45.611
And streaming.

00:15:45.611 --> 00:15:48.447
I don't personally feel that
music will be monetizable

00:15:48.447 --> 00:15:51.056
in a very foreseeable way.

00:15:51.056 --> 00:15:54.467
I think that we should focus 
on musicians as brands and

00:15:54.467 --> 00:15:57.089
we're lucky enough to use 
music as our brand builder,

00:15:57.089 --> 00:15:59.659
as our calling card and the
future of the music business is

00:15:59.659 --> 00:16:01.460
learning to build 
brands around artists.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:04.714
The artists get 
to have equity in.

00:16:04.714 --> 00:16:08.334
- Yep. You know, obviously the
monetizing of live event.

00:16:08.334 --> 00:16:11.304
So I think access is 
where all the magic is

00:16:11.304 --> 00:16:12.638
'cause it's the 
limited resource.

00:16:12.638 --> 00:16:13.306
- Mhmmm.
- Right?

00:16:13.306 --> 00:16:15.141
So whether that means 
in a show or one on ones

00:16:15.141 --> 00:16:16.776
or the brands they touch.

00:16:16.776 --> 00:16:18.644
I mean look, it's funny 
here you go with the

00:16:18.644 --> 00:16:20.780
brand move of the equity thing.
- Mhmmm.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.616
- When you think about the
economics 50 Cent made on just

00:16:23.616 --> 00:16:26.319
his sponsorship deal of Vitamin
Water let alone what you're

00:16:26.319 --> 00:16:27.653
seeing now where 
you're, you know,

00:16:27.653 --> 00:16:29.388
celebrities and 
musicians are getting

00:16:29.388 --> 00:16:32.191
5, 10, 15, 30% of a 
business before it launches on

00:16:32.191 --> 00:16:33.459
the back of their brand.

00:16:33.459 --> 00:16:35.294
It's a very entrepreneurial
answer but it's the truth.

00:16:35.294 --> 00:16:38.264
It's a race to the bottom of
control of those economics.

00:16:38.264 --> 00:16:38.878
- Yeah.

00:16:38.878 --> 00:16:40.132
- Andy?
- [Andy] Cool.

00:16:40.132 --> 00:16:42.106
- [Voiceover] Will asks, 
"How does Jewel feel about

00:16:42.106 --> 00:16:45.605
"social media access when it 
comes to celebrity privacy?"

00:16:45.605 --> 00:16:46.305
- That's a good one.

00:16:46.305 --> 00:16:48.107
- I love it. 
I'm in control of it.

00:16:48.107 --> 00:16:49.542
I've always really welcomed it.

00:16:49.542 --> 00:16:51.444
I've lived my 
life with transparency.

00:16:51.444 --> 00:16:53.446
I hide nothing.

00:16:54.881 --> 00:16:57.416
That said, I always honor--
- We love Jewel.

00:16:57.416 --> 00:16:59.914
(group laughter)

00:16:59.914 --> 00:17:04.074
You're giving all the answers
that nobody, nobody else says.

00:17:04.074 --> 00:17:05.400
Yeah and what's weird is it's

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.993
one thing say it that 
came and grew from it.

00:17:07.993 --> 00:17:10.563
You were real, real 
famous when it came along so

00:17:10.563 --> 00:17:12.464
it's an even more 
impressive answer.

00:17:12.464 --> 00:17:15.067
You know, I was a byproduct and
benefited from the transparency

00:17:15.067 --> 00:17:15.774
and grew from there.
- Yeah.

00:17:15.774 --> 00:17:18.271
- But for you to 
be where you were and

00:17:18.271 --> 00:17:21.363
love it speaks to 
that rare authenticity.

00:17:21.363 --> 00:17:23.910
- Well, I also was able, that's
funny I was put in a college

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:27.025
textbook from when 
the grassroot marketers,

00:17:27.025 --> 00:17:29.415
one of the four founders of
grassroots marketing online.

00:17:29.415 --> 00:17:30.082
- Sure.

00:17:30.082 --> 00:17:31.417
- It wasn't because of me. 
It was my fans.

00:17:31.417 --> 00:17:34.020
And it was the early days of the
internet but it was the reason

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:35.187
I broke through grunge.

00:17:35.187 --> 00:17:37.248
- But your fans, I was there.

00:17:37.248 --> 00:17:38.787
I was doing the 
Wine Library thing.

00:17:38.787 --> 00:17:39.592
It's why I was so excited.

00:17:39.592 --> 00:17:41.360
We talked a little bit 
about this the other day.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:44.500
Your fans got there and 
give a crap because of you

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:46.395
and then they took over.

00:17:46.395 --> 00:17:50.937
What my fans do now is insane
the level of love but it starts

00:17:50.937 --> 00:17:53.005
with I love them first.
- Yeah.

00:17:53.005 --> 00:17:54.373
- You have to love them first.

00:17:54.373 --> 00:17:56.208
- Yeah, music comes 
second in all honesty.

00:17:56.208 --> 00:17:58.377
I think people 
and what I've been,

00:17:58.377 --> 00:17:59.378
it's just been incredible.

00:17:59.378 --> 00:18:00.579
I have no middleman.

00:18:00.579 --> 00:18:02.782
I get to talk to my fans
directly and tell them who I am.

00:18:02.782 --> 00:18:04.750
I don't have a journalist going,

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:06.819
"You know the truth about
Jewel was blah blah blah blah."

00:18:06.819 --> 00:18:08.050
And it's not true.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:09.188
I actually get to 
tell people what's true.

00:18:09.188 --> 00:18:11.857
I get to have that direct
relationship and not to mention

00:18:11.857 --> 00:18:15.361
I should be a gift in, we're all
a gift in each other's lives.

00:18:15.361 --> 00:18:17.331
If I'm not a gift 
in the life of my fans,

00:18:17.331 --> 00:18:18.497
I am not doing my job.

00:18:18.497 --> 00:18:22.201
This isn't all about me and so
the way technology is evolved

00:18:22.201 --> 00:18:24.437
it's much easier 
for me to watch my fans,

00:18:24.437 --> 00:18:26.238
see how their 
families are doing,

00:18:26.238 --> 00:18:28.040
encourage them to be 
supporting one another.

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:31.010
I love it.
- Amazing. Andy?

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:32.545
I love it, too.

00:18:32.545 --> 00:18:35.481
- [Voiceover] Loca app asks,
"Does social media overall have

00:18:35.481 --> 00:18:39.251
"a positive or negative 
impact on mental health?"

00:18:39.251 --> 00:18:40.419
- That's a good question.

00:18:40.419 --> 00:18:41.754
- That's a great question.
- That's a great question.

00:18:41.754 --> 00:18:43.689
- Yeah, it's something 
you and I were talking about.

00:18:43.689 --> 00:18:46.993
I think that my job as an artist
has always been to look at

00:18:46.993 --> 00:18:49.628
culture and look at zeitgeist.

00:18:49.628 --> 00:18:51.230
Where is culture 
swinging and where do

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:54.300
I authentically 
intersect with that?

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.168
For me, I'm great at connection

00:18:56.168 --> 00:18:59.038
and I love the 
technological age.

00:18:59.038 --> 00:19:00.973
I love how 
accessible information is.

00:19:00.973 --> 00:19:03.676
I love that with 
education at our fingertips.

00:19:03.676 --> 00:19:05.678
I love that it's 
disrupting everything.

00:19:05.678 --> 00:19:09.815
It have the tremendous ability
to cause a distraction addiction

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:12.118
and we need to be careful with
our children and with ourselves

00:19:12.118 --> 00:19:13.052
of how we consume.

00:19:13.052 --> 00:19:15.253
Now what does that mean?
It's a very interesting topic.

00:19:15.254 --> 00:19:18.591
How do we consume in a way that
doesn't hurt our mental health?

00:19:18.591 --> 00:19:21.227
That doesn't cause 
neural pathways of addiction and

00:19:21.227 --> 00:19:22.795
distraction addiction and

00:19:22.795 --> 00:19:24.463
that's actually a 
very fascinating topic.

00:19:24.463 --> 00:19:25.831
- I think the thing that a lot
of people are talking about,

00:19:25.831 --> 00:19:27.967
Simon Sinek has a video that's
going viral on this right now.

00:19:27.967 --> 00:19:30.403
I think the question 
becomes that we never do is

00:19:30.403 --> 00:19:31.937
what was the alternative?
